 -----
 DOORS
 -----

Sterling Service doors are 90% compatible with Paragon's doors,
as of Paragon version 2.0. Many Paragon doors can be used as-is.

SETTING UP DOORS:

        Sterling uses the logical volume name of "DOORS:" for the
location of all door programs and files. You must assign this
volume name before using any of the doors.

You must place a TEXT FILE into your "BBS:" logical directory called
"doors.dat" This file is very similar to Paragon's Doors.DAT file.
It must contain the following:

        First Line............   The total number of doors
        Second Line...........   Name of the first door
        Third Line............   Access Level (BINARY COLOR LOCK FLAGS)
        Fourth Line...........   Path to & filename of door

        Fifth Line............   Name of second door (if any)
        Sixth Line............   Access level of second door
        Seventh Line..........   Path/filename of second door

        etc.

The access level is the most significant change from Paragon.
This is a BINARY DIGIT representation of the User High security
flag byte. THIS IS A COLOR LOCK, and controls access to a door. To enter
the door, a user must have at least ONE (1) of the matching color keys. If
the door has NO access flags set (this value is zero), then everyone can
enter. If a door program accesses the user's access level, the number
returned will be the COLOR KEY byte, the value of which may be zero.

The maximum number of doors supported on Sterling is 6.

 -------------------
 FILE TRANSFER SYSOP
 -------------------

The File Transfer Sysop menu is where the club sysop releases uploaded
files to the general public, and adds, deletes, or edits file headers.  The
following commands are available:

ADD FILE:  Allows the club sysop to create a new file header for a file
already on disk, allowing the keyboard sysop to add files himself to the
board.

SCAN ADD:  This searches through a directory (chosen from a menu) for any
files not currently in the Club Library.  The sysop can then elect to add
them, entering the file header information for each file as it's selected.

EDIT FILE:  Allows the club sysop to edit the file header of an existing
file.  Under this option, if the sysop doesn't create a new file message
(if such messages are active), the old message remains intact, and the EDIT
is not aborted.

DELETE FILE:  Allows the club sysop to eliminate a file from the board.

BATCH DELETE:  Allows the Sysop to delete all Club Library files that are
no longer on disk.  This is handy when deleting batches of files using a
CLI-helper utility.

NEW FILE:  New files are only available to club sysops.  This command scans
the file headers for any that haven't been approved yet, and lists them in
the manner of Downloads.  The sysop can then select and edit those he
wishes to approve.

LIST DIRECTORY:  This command lists the contents of a disk directory
(chosen from a menu), showing file dates and protection bits.

The question mark lists the available commands.

Note that the Sysop is the only one who can can see and download all
newly-uploaded files.  Until he approves the file (users can see) with
the NEW FILE command, he's the only one who can do so.

NOTE:  All sysop areas are structured different from the rest of the board.
Commands can't be changed.  Menus are hard-coded into the program and also
can't be changed.

------------
 SUPERVISOR
------------

This is the most powerful area of the board, thus access should be
restricted to a very few people.  If the user has SUPERVISOR set in his
Sysop Security flags, he must enter a Supervisor password in three tries.

(The supervisor and cli password in the demo are both "password")

Should he fail, he looses all Sysop and Color Key security access, and a
warning is left in the LOG file (if active).On successful entry, the
Supervisor is shown a brief summary of system statistics:  number of calls,
total number of messages written, total number of uploads, and the current
number of valid users.

 Pressing RETURN instead of a valid entry will return to the previous menu
level.  If done at the top of the Supervisor level, the user will be
returned to the Main Menu.  Pressing the question-mark will print the
available options.

The top level of the Supervisor area is a menu covering six areas:

	* USERFILE MAINTENANCE
	* SYSTEM MAINTENANCE
	* CLUB MAINTENANCE
	* WRITING SYSTEM MESSAGES
	* READING ELECTRONIC MAIL
	* SENDING CLI COMMANDS

 USER MAINTENANCE

This area allows the Supervisor total control over the system's Userfile.
The following options are available:

ADD USER:  Allows the Supervisor to add a user manually.

DELETE USER:  Removes a user from the board.

EDIT USER:  Allows changes to the following areas of a user's file:

	* USER NAME
	* PASSWORD
	* CITY
	* STATE
	* TELEPHONE NUMBER
	* SECURITY FLAGS (User Low, User High, and Sysop)
	* DOWNLOAD RATIO
	* DOWNLOAD ENABLE FLAGS
	* SCREEN SIZE
	* MAX MINUTES ALLOWED ON-LINE

OK NEW USER:  This option searches for all new users that haven't been
reviewed yet, and offers the Supervisor a chance to change the User
security flags.

CHANGE ALL COLOR KEYS:  This function changes the color key access of all
users.

RESET MAX TIME:  This command resets all users except user #1, the
Supervisor to the same Max Time currently given to new users.

VIEW USERS:  This allows the Supervisor to view the statistics of selected
users.

	* ALL USERS:  Lists using pattern matching on the User
                     Names)
	* ALL USERS:  Lists everyone without pausing)
	* NO SHOWS:  Lists those who have not been on in a number
                    of days.  That number of days is entered by the
                    Supervisor before searching.
	* COLOR KEY:  Lists all users with a specific color key.  The
                     Supervisor determines which color key is used for the
                    search.
	* SHOPPERS:  Lists all users who have access to the Shopping
                    Mall.

